# linux/deploy-manifest — the single truth about what lives where in a home on
# a Linux session host. Format: source target mode kind  (source relative to the
# repo root, target relative to the home root). One source may have several
# target rows. The row list was MEASURED against real homes — change it by
# measuring, not by guessing.
#
# An estate adds its own rows in its own manifest; the deploy composes the union
# and prints how many rows each side contributed. A stranger who clones this
# repo gets the mechanism's rows and nothing else.
#
# FILE NAMES ARE STILL BEING RENAMED, and one of them is now done. The session
# and job units used to be named after the estate; as of 2026-08-20 they carry a
# FUNCTION name instead — `agent-`, which belongs to neither the estate nor this
# product. That choice was deliberate: naming them after the product would have
# put the product's name into somebody's operational layer, and the next rename
# would drag thirty live timers along with it.
#
# The rename is only half an operation. New unit files can be DEPLOYED, but
# switching a host over means disabling the old instances and enabling the new
# ones per home — a service verb the deploy path refuses by design (see
# test/deploy-policy.test.sh: daemon-reload and nothing else). Until a host has
# had that done by hand, it runs the old units; the rows below describe what the
# deploy WRITES, never what a host currently runs.
#
# What remains named in the estate's own language: a directory and two scripts,
# all Swedish rather than estate-specific, and none of them load-bearing for a
# running unit. Those are a rename with no operational half.

