# THE SPAWN LIBRARIES TRAVEL WITH THE SUPERVISOR, and both of them or neither.
# The supervisor refuses to start a session when either is absent (rc 78) --
# a deployed supervisor that could not read its spawn policy would otherwise
# fall back to the legacy command line and hand every session whatever its
# checkout happens to declare, which is the one outcome the policy exists to
# prevent. Same directory as registry.sh, because that is where the supervisor
# looks: it sources these from the directory it found registry.sh in.
lib/mcprender.sh                   scripts/lib/mcprender.sh             755  lib
lib/mcpspawn.sh                    scripts/lib/mcpspawn.sh              755  lib
# THE LIVENESS SEAM TRAVELS WITH THE REGISTRY, and the desk is what made that
# visible. The desk's producer refuses to run without it (rc 78) and takes the
# seam from THE SAME DIRECTORY the registry library came from, by design: an
# override that aimed one at one tree and the other at a second would measure
# one estate and describe another. A home with registry.sh and no liveness.sh
# is therefore a home whose snapshot round exits 78 every five minutes, into a
# journal, while the desk keeps serving the last generation it managed to
# write.
lib/liveness.sh                    scripts/lib/liveness.sh              755  lib
# THE VISIBILITY RULE TRAVELS WITH THE REGISTRY THE SAME WAY. The desk's
# producer sources it from the registry library's own directory, right after
# liveness.sh, and refuses the same way when it is absent. A home with
# registry.sh and no visibility.sh fails the same way liveness.sh did above —
# measured 2026-09-08 on a deployed home.
lib/visibility.sh                  scripts/lib/visibility.sh            755  lib
# bus clients + browser (executable locations — the loneliness sweep's core targets)
linux/bus-send                     bin/bus-send                         755  bin
linux/bus-read                     bin/bus-read                         755  bin
