# THE DESK: a read-only page per person, served on a unix socket in the hub's
# own home. Two halves, and both of them land here.
#
# THE PRODUCER (snapshot.sh + filter.jq) runs where the estate is - it reads
# the registry and the liveness seam, and writes one already-filtered file per
# principal. The filter is the security boundary and it is a manifest row like
# any other: a home running an older filter.jq is a home whose files carry more
# than the current rules allow, and nothing downstream would notice.
#
# THE SERVER (serve.mjs + render.mjs) IS SHIPPED BECAUSE THE HUB SERVES. It is
# the one process here that a network reaches, and it runs from the deployed
# home like everything else a unit starts - the unit's ExecStart names
# %h/scripts/desk/serve.mjs, so a home without these two files has a service
# that fails to start on every boot.
#
# NO package.json ROW. A .mjs file is an ES module by its extension, so nothing
# in a deployed home needs one to load these two: the desk directory holds no
# .js file for a `type` field to speak for, and the only other thing the file
# carries is a test script naming desk/test, which this manifest deliberately
# does not deploy. The row shipped a file with no reader in the home it landed
# in - measured 2026-09-08.
#
# THE BRIDGES ARE 755 BECAUSE THEY ARE SPAWNED, not sourced. desk-paths answers
# "where does the desk live" for both languages, and principal-for-login is the
# gate itself: the server EXECUTES it once per request to turn a login into a
# principal, and checks its executable bit at startup precisely because a
# deploy that dropped the bit would otherwise leave every person looking at
# "no desk for this login" forever.
#
# NOT desk/test/ - see test/deploy-manifest.test.sh, which guards that.
#
# BIN/STEWARD SHIPS WITH THE DESK because the producer calls it: snapshot.sh
# runs `steward mcp surface` for the mcp section of every principal's file. A
# home without this row serves a desk whose mcp section is quietly null - the
# call fails, the RAW document falls back to its default, and nothing marks
# the page as degraded. It resolves lib/ and desk/ relative to its own parent,
# so the deployed layout (scripts/bin/steward beside scripts/lib and
# scripts/desk) is the layout it already expects. This is the FIRST row that
# puts the command-line client into other people's homes, and it grants nobody
# anything: the client reads the registry and spawns as whoever invokes it, so
# a home that now holds it can do exactly what that account could already do
# by other means - what the row buys is that the producer's `steward mcp
# surface` call resolves instead of failing silently.
bin/steward                      scripts/bin/steward                  755  scripts
desk/snapshot.sh                 scripts/desk/snapshot.sh             755  scripts
desk/filter.jq                   scripts/desk/filter.jq               644  scripts
desk/serve.mjs                   scripts/desk/serve.mjs               644  scripts
desk/render.mjs                  scripts/desk/render.mjs              644  scripts
desk/SCHEMA.md                   scripts/desk/SCHEMA.md               644  scripts
